    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191150": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure-Track, Biomedical Engineering: Biomaterials or Biomechanics",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Engineering and Computer Science: ECS Buffer: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Waco, Texas The Biomedical Engineering (BME) program at Baylor University seeks candidates for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor beginning August 2027. Baylor is investing in the rapid growth of the BME program, which emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration across engineering, medicine, and the health sciences. The successful candidate will pursue excellence in teaching, research, and scholarship grounded in faith. They will play a foundational role in shaping the curriculum, research culture, and future direction of the program while strengthening Baylor’s distinctive Christian mission. We seek candidates with expertise in biomaterials or biomechanics who employ experimental, computational, or combined approaches to advance fundamental understanding or translational biomedical technologies. We especially encourage candidates whose work complements and expands existing strengths in biosensors, bioelectronics, medical devices, and artificial intelligence. A Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, or a closely related field is required. Candidates should also demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching, the potential to build a strong externally funded research program, the ability to mentor graduate and undergraduate students, and effective communication and organizational skills. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Review of applications will begin on October 31, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after December 31, 2026, can be considered. The BME program is currently administered within the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering in the School of Engineering and Computer Science. The program offers BS and MS degrees, with a Ph.D. program in development. Program faculty and students are located in the Rogers Engineering and Computer Science Building and the Baylor Research and Innovation Collaborative (BRIC) , a 330,000 square-foot interdisciplinary research facility. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas , and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor’s new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering, Bioengineering, or a closely related field Demonstrated potential to develop and sustain an active, externally funded research program (as PI or co-PI) Preferred Strong record of research excellence and teaching effectiveness, with expertise in biomaterials, biomechanics, or closely related areas Research that complements existing strengths in biosensors, bioelectronics, medical devices, and artificial intelligence Evidence of leadership in research, teaching, interdisciplinary collaboration, or program development The following documents are required: Cover letter that describes the applicant’s research vision and fit with the position Curriculum Vitae Statement of research interests Statement of teaching interests related to Baylor’s programs Contact information for at least three professional references Academic transcript of highest degree earned (unofficial is acceptable) Religious Affiliation Form (found in Interfolio) The following documents are optional: Publications"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191515": {
      "title": "Heilmeier Endowed Chair Professor, Tenured, Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Engineering and Computer Science: ECS Buffer: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Waco, TX One of the most prestigious faculty appointments in the School of Engineering and Computer Science, the Heilmeier Endowed Chair is intended for an internationally recognized scholar whose research, leadership, and vision will help shape the future direction of electrical and computer engineering at Baylor University. The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at Baylor University seeks qualified candidates for a tenured, endowed chair position with the rank of Full Professor beginning in August 2027. Successful candidates must have a Ph.D. in ECE, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field. The successful candidate will demonstrate a record of sustained scholarly distinction and a compelling vision for advancing research excellence, innovation, and societal impact. The Chair will serve as a catalyst for faculty collaboration, graduate student success, strategic partnerships, and national and international visibility for Baylor University’s engineering enterprise. The candidate will also show a record of excellence in teaching both undergraduate and graduate students, have a proven track record of supporting a strong, research program, and have effective communication and organizational skills. Selected candidates will be expected to perform academic duties associated with degree programs offered by the department and to initiate and carry out research, developing and sustaining a highly visible, externally funded research enterprise that includes leadership of large-scale research initiatives, collaborative programs, and multidisciplinary team. The endowed chair holder is expected to provide intellectual and strategic leadership within the department, school, and university. Areas of impact may include mentoring junior faculty, fostering interdisciplinary collaborations, helping shape future faculty hiring priorities, strengthening graduate education, and expanding Baylor’s national and international research reputation. Baylor’s ABET-accredited ECE department currently has 18 tenured/tenure-track faculty including the Mearse Chair in Biological and Biomedical Engineering, and 4 non-tenure-track faculty members. The faculty are internationally recognized in research areas including power and energy systems; wireless and photonics systems, semiconductors, RF, and devices; AI and machine learning; advanced computing, cyber-physical systems and bioengineering. The department is housed within the Rogers Engineering Building and the Baylor Research and Innovation Collaborative (BRIC; https://bric.research.baylor.edu/ ). The department offers BS (including degrees in ECE and Computer Engineering), MS, and PhD degrees in Electrical and Computer Engineering. Additional information regarding the department, including its mission, is available at https://www.ecs.baylor.edu/ece . The successful candidate will have opportunities to collaborate across Baylor’s growing research ecosystem, including the Baylor Research and Innovation Collaborative (BRIC), initiatives in materials and devices, energy systems, artificial intelligences, advanced computing, health-related technologies, and emerging engineering disciplines such as aerospace engineering and biomedical engineering. Candidates whose research can build bridges across multiple academic units are especially encouraged to apply. About Baylor University: Located in Waco, Texas, Baylor University is the oldest college in Texas. With a population of around 21,000 students, Baylor is one of the top universities in the nation, having been named an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also on the honor roll of the \"Great Colleges to Work For\" from The Chronicle of Higher Education. Baylor offers competitive salaries and benefits while giving faculty and staff the chance to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. Our strategic plan guides the University as we continue to live up to Baylor's mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Distinguishing Expectations of the Heilmeier Endowed Chair The holder of the Heilmeier Endowed Chair will be expected to: Serve as a visible ambassador for Baylor University nationally and internationally. Lead transformative research initiatives aligned with instructional priorities. Assist in recruiting outstanding faculty, graduate students, and postdoctoral scholars. Foster interdisciplinary collaborations across the university. Advance Baylor’s visibility among federal sponsors and industry partners. Contribute to the development of future research centers and strategic initiatives. Qualifications · Education & Experience: A Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering, Electrical Engineering, or a closely related field, along with a minimum of 10 years of professional experience post Ph.D. degree. · Research Leadership & Center Creation: A proven track record of visionary leadership. Strong preference will be given to candidates with demonstrated success in leading major research initiatives, centers, institutes, laboratories, or large-scale collaborative programs involving multiple investigators, institutions, and external stakeholders. Experience securing substantial federal, industrial, or foundation support is highly desired. · Scholarly Excellence: A distinguished record of high-impact scholarship, including nationally or internationally recognized publications and a strong history of securing significant external research funding. · Teaching & Mentorship: Demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentoring students, postdoctoral scholars, and early-career faculty. A strong record of graduate student placement, research mentorship, and talent development is highly desirable. · National and International Recognition: Evidence of national or international leadership in the field through major awards, fellow status in professional societies, editorial responsibilities, invited keynote presentations, professional service, or other indicators of scholarly distinction. · Partnership Development: Demonstrated ability to cultivate productive partnerships with federal agencies, industry, national laboratories, healthcare organizations, nonprofits, or other external stakeholders that enhance research impact and educational opportunities. Please provide the following documents through Interfolio: Cover letter CV (Curriculum Vitae) Vision statement Statement of research interests Statement of teaching interests related to Baylor's programs Contact information for at least three professional references Academic transcript of highest degree earned (Unofficial is accepted) Religious Affiliation Form (found in Interfolio) Applicants are strongly encouraged to submit materials by the priority deadline of October 31, 2026 , prior to the start of first-round interviews on November 15 . Final applications must be received by December 31, 2026 , for full consideration."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190224": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Tenure Track or Tenured, Materials Science and Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Engineering and Computer Science: ECS Buffer: Mechanical Engineering Location: Waco, Texas The Department of Mechanical Engineering (ME) in the School of Engineering and Computer Science at Baylor University seeks candidates for a tenure-track or tenured position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor to start in August 2027. This position is part of a multi-year expansion of faculty in materials science and engineering across the School of Engineering and Computer Science and the College of Arts and Sciences, reflecting a strategic University investment in the area. Details of the position can be found in ID: 188268. We seek an innovative and motivated individual who will support the new multidisciplinary Materials Science and Engineering PhD program while also serving the broader interests of the Mechanical Engineering department. The successful candidate will be expected to strengthen and elevate the quality and breadth of research and teaching in the department, with an emphasis on materials science and engineering. We welcome applicants with either experimental, computational or a combination of both skills. Research interests may include, but are not limited to: (1) additive manufacturing; (2) composite materials; (3) materials for extreme environments, such as ceramics and alloys (4) non-destructive evaluation; (5) advanced materials characterization, including multiscale characterization of engineering materials in extreme environments ; (6) experimental and computational mechanics; (7) modeling and simulation of materials processing, including AI-guided approaches; and (8) data-driven and machine learning methods for materials design and discovery. Responsibilities include active research, undergraduate and graduate teaching, course curriculum development, and professional service. A competitive startup package will accompany the position. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. The Department of Mechanical Engineering: Baylor’s ABET-accredited ME program currently has 19 tenured/tenure-track faculty members including the Kenneth and Celia Carlile Endowed Chair, a research professor, three clinical faculty members, and five lecturer faculty members. The faculty are internationally recognized in research areas including Biomechanics, Biomedical Sensors, Additive and Advanced Materials, Ceramic and Polymeric Composites, Non-destructive Evaluation, Sustainable Energy and Power Systems, Combustion, Aerodynamics and Thermophysics, and Interfacial Fluid Mechanics. The department is housed within the Rogers Building and the Baylor Research and Innovation Collaborative (BRIC) (see www.baylor.edu/bric ). The department offers BS, MS, and PhD degrees in Mechanical Engineering and a BS in Aerospace Engineering. The department, along with Electrical Engineering, Physics and Chemistry manage Baylor’s MS and PhD in Materials Science and Engineering. The department, jointly with the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, oversees the Pre-Engineering program and offers a BS in Engineering and Biomedical Engineering, MS in Biomedical Engineering, and Master of Engineering degrees. Enrollment during AY 2025-2026 was 209 pre-engineering students, 231 ME undergraduates, and 45 full-time ME graduate students. The Materials Science and Engineering Program: Faculty in the Department of Mechanical Engineering contribute to Baylor’s newly established MS and PhD program in Materials Science and Engineering, a university-wide initiative that fosters interdisciplinary collaboration across departments. This research-intensive program is strategically aligned with national priorities in Advanced Structural and Multifunctional Materials and Optical and Quantum Materials. Faculty engaged in this program have the opportunity to mentor masters and doctoral students, participate in cross-disciplinary research teams, and shape a growing area of scholarship at Baylor. The program is supported by Baylor’s advanced experimental and computational research infrastructure and reflects the university’s commitment to expanding its research footprint in emerging advanced materials technologies. Faculty in the program collaborate with the Center for Microscopy and Imaging (CMI) (https://bsb.research.baylor.edu/core-research-centers/center-microscopy-and-imaging), which provides electron, atomic force, and confocal microscopy along with sample preparation for materials characterization. They also collaborate with the Point-of-Need Innovations (PONI) Center (https://poni.research.baylor.edu) in the BRIC, an interdisciplinary research and development center spanning materials, manufacturing, engineering design, business management, and logistics, whose more than 5,000 square feet of laboratory space includes a 1,500-square-foot ITAR laboratory. The BRIC also hosts the Materials Testing and Characterization Core (MTCC), an ISO 17025 accredited testing facility staffed by PhD-level research scientists and technicians. Current enrollment is 41 full-time MS and PhD seeking graduate students. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Baylor University is a private not-for-profit university affiliated with the Baptist General Convention of Texas. As an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity employer, Baylor is committed to compliance with all applicable anti-discrimination laws, including those regarding age, race, color, sex, national origin, pregnancy status, military service, genetic information, and disability. As a religious educational institution, Baylor is lawfully permitted to consider an applicant’s religion as a selection criterion. Baylor encourages women, minorities, veterans, and individuals with disabilities to apply. Requirements include: 1) an earned doctorate in Materials Science and Engineering, Mechanical Engineering or a closely related field , 2) outstanding English communication skills, 3) a commitment to teaching excellence, 4) demonstrated research achievement and publication record commensurate with appointment at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor, and 5) a commitment to professional activities. Preferred: 1) post-doctoral experience or other industrial experience, and 2) applicant research topics complementing existing areas of departmental expertise and intersecting the strategic areas identified in Baylor’s strategic plan. Review of applicants will begin on October 31, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after December 31, 2026 can be considered. Applications must include : 1) a cover letter, 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) transcript of highest degree earned 4) an individualized statement of teaching interests, 5) an individualized statement of research plans related to Baylor's programs, and 6) contact information for at least three professional references. Additionally, applicants must also complete the Baylor University Religious Affiliation Form (RAF) to be considered for the position. Letters of reference will be required of finalists."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190439": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Aerospace Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Engineering and Computer Science: ECS Buffer: Mechanical Engineering Location: Waco, Texas The Department of Mechanical Engineering in the School of Engineering and Computer Science at Baylor University seeks to fill a tenure track position in Aerospace Engineering at the rank of Assistant Professor to start on August 1, 2027. Responsibilities include developing and maintaining an externally sponsored research program, undergraduate and graduate teaching, course and curriculum development, and professional service. This position is open to candidates in engineering with teaching and research expertise in either astronautics or aeronautics. On the astronautics side, this includes but is not limited to space propulsion systems, human space flight and space exploration, space environments, orbital mechanics, space autonomy, and spacecraft design. On the aeronautics side, this includes but is not limited to CFD, aerodynamics, aircraft propulsion, sustainable aviation, advanced air mobility, and hypersonics. As of the fall semester of 2026, the Department of Mechanical Engineering launched a B.S. in Aerospace Engineering program. The candidate selected for the position will be expected to develop and maintain a strong, funded research program while assisting in delivering the new program. Baylor’s Department of Mechanical Engineering currently has 19 tenured/tenure-track faculty members including the Kenneth and Celia Carlile Endowed Chair, a research professor, three clinical faculty members, and five lecturer faculty members. The faculty are internationally recognized in research areas including Biomechanics, Biomedical Sensors, Additive and Advanced Materials, Ceramic and Polymeric Composites, Sustainable Energy and Power Systems, Combustion, Aerodynamics and Thermophysics, and Interfacial Fluid Mechanics. The department is housed within the Rogers Building and the Baylor Research and Innovation Collaborative (BRIC) (see www.baylor.edu/bric). The department offers BS, MS, and PhD degrees in Mechanical Engineering and B.S. in Aerospace Engineering. Enrollment during AY 2025-2026 was 209 pre-engineering students, 231 ME undergraduates, and 45 full-time graduate students. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required qualifications: an earned doctorate in aerospace engineering or a closely related field outstanding communication skills a commitment to teaching excellence in areas relevant to the position and, for candidates with prior teaching experience, a record of successful teaching demonstrated potential for research achievement in areas relevant to the position Preferred qualification: potential for leadership in the new Aerospace Engineering Program Review of applications will begin on September 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled . Applications received after December 31, 2026 cannot be considered. Applications must include: a cover letter/letter of application a current curriculum vita (CV) a transcript (official or unofficial) of the highest degree earned or in progress an individualized statement of teaching interests an individualized statement of research plans related to Baylor's programs contact information for at least three professional references. Additionally, applicants must complete the Baylor University Religious Affiliation Form (RAF) in Interfolio to be considered for the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188136": {
      "title": "Professor and Chair, School of Earth and Environmental Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Arts and Sciences Location: Waco, TX Baylor University seeks a visionary leader and accomplished scholar to serve as the inaugural Chair of the School of Earth and Environmental Sciences (SEES). The Chair will play a central role in developing and advancing an integrated vision for research, education, and program expansion aligned with Baylor’s goals as a nationally recognized R1 institution. The Chair will demonstrate a commitment to Baylor's mission as a Christian research university. The new School, established in June 2026, is being formed through the integration of the Departments of Environmental Science and Geosciences and builds on established strengths across a broad range of earth and environmental systems research and teaching. These include environmental health and toxicology, environmental chemistry, ecosystem and atmospheric modeling, hydrological and surface processes, biogeosciences, climate and paleoclimate, energy geosciences, environmental law and policy, and solid earth and planetary sciences. Committed to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education, SEES is expanding its academic programs and interdisciplinary collaborations to drive continued growth and impact. Reporting to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences, the Chair will provide strategic, collaborative, and entrepreneurial leadership in shaping SEES identity, advancing its research and academic programs, and increasing its national and international visibility. The successful candidate will lead SEES during a period of anticipated growth, including the expansion of academic programs and increased interdisciplinary collaboration across Earth and Environmental Sciences, while supporting a commitment to Baylor’s mission, as is expected for a Christian research university. The Chair will work collaboratively with faculty, staff, and university leadership to: Develop, articulate, and implement a strategic vision for SEES aligned with Baylor’s priorities as a Carnegie-designated R1 institution Advance research excellence and external funding Strengthen undergraduate and graduate education Foster interdisciplinary initiatives within SEES and across the University Recruit, mentor, and retain faculty with strong records of research, teaching, and service Enhance the School’s national and international visibility Oversee academic programs, budgeting, personnel, and administrative operations Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of \"Great Colleges to Work For\" by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. REQUIRED Earned doctorate in Earth Science, Environmental Sciences, Environmental Health, Geoscience, or a closely related field Demonstrate scholarship and record of external funding consistent with an appointment as a tenured Professor Experience in academic leadership or administration Strong commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring PREFERRED Demonstrated ability to foster collaborative relationships across disciplines and with internal and external partners Ability to develop and articulate a clear vision for advancing research and academic programs in Earth and Environmental Sciences A track record of successfully developing and implementing new initiatives To apply, please submit the following materials through Interfolio: Letter of application addressing interest and qualification, including the following sections: Statement of research interests and accomplishments Statement of teaching philosophy and accomplishments Statement of leadership experience and vision for the School of Earth and Environmental Sciences Copy of the official transcript from the highest-degree granting institution Curriculum vita Names and contact information for three professional references Selected applicants will be contacted to complete a required, self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form. Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after November 30, 2026 will be considered . For questions regarding the position, please contact search chair, Dr. Kevin Chambliss, at Kevin_Chambliss@baylor.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188409": {
      "title": "Associate to Full Professor and Chair, Department of Journalism, Public Relations & New Media",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Arts and Sciences Location: Waco, TX Baylor University invites applications and nominations for the position of Department Chair of Journalism, Public Relations & New Media in the College of Arts & Sciences. The successful candidate will be eligible for appointment with tenure at the rank of Professor or Associate Professor (advanced) and will bring a record of leadership, collaboration, and commitment to excellence in teaching, research, and service. Baylor University is a Research 1 (R1) institution, and the department seeks a leader who understands and supports a dynamic balance of teaching excellence, impactful scholarship, and professional engagement. The Department Chair serves as the academic and administrative leader of the department and reports to the Dean of the College of Arts & Sciences. The Chair will articulate and advance a clear vision for the department’s future, support faculty and student success, and represent the department to internal and external stakeholders. The department seeks a visionary, collaborative leader who will empower faculty to prepare highly competent, socially conscious, and engaged communication professionals. The Chair will foster a collegial and inclusive environment while advancing the department’s strategic priorities in teaching, research, and professional engagement. Key responsibilities include: Providing strategic leadership and vision, including long-range planning and program development Supporting excellence in undergraduate and graduate education Overseeing faculty recruitment, mentoring, evaluation, and development Managing budgets, resources, and operations effectively Promoting research, creative activity, and external funding opportunities Leading curricular innovation and assessment Building and maintaining relationships with students, parents, alumni, donors, and industry partners Representing the department within the College, University, and broader professional communities Fostering a culture of collaborative governance, transparency, and inclusivity Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. About the Department: Baylor University’s Department of Journalism, Public Relations, and New Media prepares students for leadership in a quickly changing media world. The department emphasizes ethical practices, critical thinking, professional skills, and innovative storytelling and strategic communication methods. We offer three undergraduate majors: advertising and public relations; multimedia journalism, with tracks in writing and reporting, broadcast journalism, and visual media; and sports media, with tracks in writing and reporting, as well as advertising and public relations. These programs replace the department’s former journalism major, which included five concentrations: news editorial, public relations, advertising, photojournalism, and broadcast journalism. Each program focuses on practical experience and includes a capstone portfolio and internships. We are ACEJMC accredited and will celebrate our 100th anniversary in 2027. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of \"Great Colleges to Work For\" by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Journalism, Communication, Media, or a closely related field Qualifications for appointment with tenure at Baylor University at the rank of Professor or Associate Professor nearing promotion Demonstrated administrative or leadership experience, including planning, personnel management, and budgeting Strong interpersonal, written, and oral communication skills, with a collaborative and collegial leadership style Demonstrated ability to work effectively with faculty, administrators, students, alumni, and external stakeholders A record of scholarly or creative accomplishment consistent with expectations for tenure at Baylor Commitment to excellence in teaching and student mentorship Support for faculty research, including grant-writing and mentoring Commitment to Baylor University’s Christian mission and institutional values Preferred Qualifications: Professional experience in journalism, public relations, advertising, or related media fields (preferably 5–10 years, though not required) Experience fostering industry partnerships, internships, and career pathways for students Demonstrated success in strategic planning and program development Experience mentoring faculty at different career stages Understanding of the rapidly evolving media and communication landscape, including emerging technologies To apply, please submit the following materials through Interfolio: Letter of application addressing interest and qualification, including the following sections: Statement of research interests and accomplishments Statement of teaching philosophy and accomplishments Statement of leadership experience and vision for the department Copy of the official transcript from the highest-degree granting institution Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for at least three professional references Self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026 , and continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after October 25, 2026 will be considered . For questions regarding the position, please contact search chair, Dr. James Kendrick, at James_Kendrick@baylor.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191624": {
      "title": "Chair and Professor, Tenured, Department of Curriculum & Instruction",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Education Location: Waco, Texas RESPONSIBILITIES: The department chair is C&I’s chief academic and administrative officer, overseeing and promoting the department’s research productivity and impact, academic programs, service, and outreach. The chair provides strategic leadership, insightful evaluation, and sound mentoring and professional development for faculty (all ranks) and staff. The chair is responsible for leading the department and enhancing its relationships with other departments in the Moody School of Education (SOE), with the university at large, and with local school districts. As a member of the School's leadership team, the chair reports directly to the dean. The department chair is responsible for: ● Providing strategic leadership for the C&I Department, advancing its mission across research, teaching, and service. ● Leading the department in strengthening its national and international reputation as a research-intensive unit, including support for high-impact scholarship and research growth. ● Developing and implementing a shared vision that reflects the department’s intellectual breadth and aligns with Moody SOE and university priorities. ● Supporting faculty success across all ranks through mentoring, professional development, promotion guidance, and leadership cultivation. ● Recruiting, retaining, and supporting an intellectually diverse faculty, recognizing excellence across research, teaching, and service. ● Strengthening and expanding academic programs, including doctoral education, student mentoring, and research training. ● Advancing educator preparation programs and cultivating strong PK–12 partnerships that support clinically rich practice and community engagement. ● Overseeing department operations, including: ○ Strategic budget development and management ○ Course scheduling and instructional quality ○ Policy development and implementation ○ Personnel management, including performance reviews. ● Collaborating with Moody SOE leadership to advance strategic initiatives and institutional priorities. SALARY AND BEGINNING DATE: The salary will be commensurate with qualifications and professional experience. The ideal date of appointment is June or July 2027 but could be negotiated. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H1-B petition. DEPARTMENT: The Department of Curriculum & Instruction (C&I) encompasses diverse educator preparation contexts, scholarly traditions, research methodologies, and disciplinary perspectives. The department prepares professional educators, scholars, and leaders for transformative impact in teaching, research, and policy. Faculty and graduates contribute to advancing knowledge and practice in ways that address complex educational challenges with innovation, creativity, and rigor. As Moody SOE's largest producer of K–12 educators, C&I plays a central role in undergraduate teacher preparation. The department also administers robust master’s and doctoral programs that contribute to Baylor’s growing research profile and national visibility. C&I is distinguished by its emphasis on clinically rich practice and strong partnerships with PK–12 schools, districts, and community organizations. These relationships contribute to innovative educator preparation approaches, extensive field-based learning opportunities, and research-practice collaborations that extend the MSOE’s impact beyond the university. The C&I department faculty is widely recognized for innovative pedagogy and exemplary instruction. Since 2021, four members have been honored with university-wide Outstanding Faculty Awards for teaching and outreach, and in each of the past three years, a C&I faculty member has been designated a Baylor Fellow, a distinction reserved for those who exemplify pedagogical innovation and inspirational teaching. Nationally, two C&I faculty members have been selected as Spencer Fellows (in 2021 and 2026), underscoring the department’s scholarly impact. The department’s student success is equally notable. In seven of the past nine years, a Baylor C&I graduate has been named Texas Student Teacher of the Year, and a Baylor C&I graduate earned the National Student Teacher of the Year distinction in 2019, 2022, and 2024. To learn more about the department, please visit: ci.moody.baylor.edu . SCHOOL: Founded in 1919 and endowed by the Moody Foundation in 2025, Baylor University’s Moody School of Education prepares leaders for a changing world, offering degrees at every level for careers in education, leadership, and human development. The School’s programs are nationally recognized for exemplary preparation of research scholars, educational leaders, innovators, and clinicians, distinguished by a blend of theory and robust clinical experiences. In recent years, Moody SOE has achieved significant gains in research productivity, external funding, and scholarly impact, reflecting Baylor’s Carnegie R1 designation and the School’s growing research stature. Its applied research and outreach activities impact thousands of communities, families, schools, and students annually. With a distinguished faculty, highly cited scholarship, and a growing portfolio of innovative graduate programs, the School is addressing pressing societal challenges with clarity and conviction. Moody SOE includes more than 70 full-time faculty across four departments (Curriculum & Instruction, Educational Leadership, Educational Psychology, and Professional Online Learning) and offers a comprehensive portfolio of undergraduate through doctoral programs. The School is also home to three nationally recognized research centers that serve schools, communities, and families across the United States and abroad: the Baylor Center for Disability and Flourishing, the Baylor Center for School Leadership, and the Baylor Center for Talent Identification Programs.. Through strategic faculty hiring, expanding residential and online offerings, and continued program innovation, Moody SOE is experiencing strong momentum and opportunity, creating a compelling environment for aspiring department leaders. To learn more about the Moody School of Education, please visit moody.baylor.edu . UNIVERSITY: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification of Institutions of Higher Education®. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the university as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Select Baylor Accolades: R1 Carnegie Classification (reaffirmed in 2025) U.S. News & World Reports Recognition and National Rankings: First-Year Experience (#4), Learning Communities (#9), Undergraduate Teaching (#27), Undergraduate Research/Creative Projects (#40), National Universities (#88) Largest Protestant university in the world holding R1 classification 14:1 student-to-faculty ratio and average class size of 26 94% of undergraduate courses taught by Baylor faculty Baylor welcomes students from all 50 states and more than 90 countries to study a broad range of degrees among its 12 nationally recognized academic divisions. Baylor’s curriculum includes 126 baccalaureate programs, 79 master’s programs, 47 doctoral programs (including the JD), the Education Specialist program, and the Master of Laws program. REQUIRED QUALIFICATIONS: ● Doctorate in a discipline represented within the department or a closely related field with academic credentials sufficient to merit the rank of tenured full professor at an R1 institution. ● Ability to advance Baylor’s strategic priorities and current strategic plan, Baylor In Deeds . ● Proven record as an academic leader with relevant administrative experience. ● Nationally recognized scholarship with a strong refereed research publication record. ● Demonstrated ability to support, secure, or facilitate externally funded research, commensurate with disciplinary expectations. ● Demonstrated commitment to collaborative and innovative approaches that advance teaching and research. ● Sustained experience with and strong commitment to educator preparation program(s) with active PK-12 school partnerships. ● Ability to collaboratively develop and advance a shared departmental vision that promotes excellence, innovation, and collegiality. ● Strong commitment to transparent communication, shared governance, and faculty engagement. ● Demonstrated ability to support innovative approaches to recruitment, development, and support of undergraduate and graduate programs. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: ● Experience in a formal academic leadership role (e.g., department chair, program director, associate dean) in an R1 or comparable environment. ● Experience leading educator preparation program(s) with active PK-12 school partnerships. ● Demonstrated success in faculty development, including mentoring across career stages and supporting promotion and advancement. ● Evidence of excellence in teaching and commitment to student success across undergraduate and graduate programs. ● Experience fostering interdisciplinary collaboration and innovative academic programming ● Leadership in national and international professional organizations related to the Department’s disciplines. SUBMISSION DEADLINE: Review of applications will begin October 1, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after February 1, 2027 can be considered. APPLICATION PROCEDURE: Candidates should include the following in their application materials. REQUIRED: 1. Letter of interest that addresses the position’s qualifications and responsibilities and includes a statement of personal faith and potential fit with Baylor’s mission 2. A curriculum vitae 3. Transcripts documenting the terminal degree 4. Contact information for three to five professional references. Note: Reference checks will only be initiated for those applicants who advance to the finalist phase (i.e., on-campus interviews). All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form (RAF) in Interfolio. If you have questions regarding the position, please contact search committee chair, Dr. Grant Morgan ( grant_morgan@baylor.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193551": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Part-Time Temporary Non-Tenure Track, Baylor Interdisciplinary Core",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Honors Location: Waco, Texas The Baylor Interdisciplinary Core (BIC), one of four programs within the Honors College, seeks to fill temporary, non-tenure track, part-time teaching positions.  The teaching assignment will vary in terms of percentage of time (generally 25% to 50% time)dependent on the needs of the department.  This is a pooled search, and applications will be kept active for consideration for all appropriate vacancies within the BIC.  For more details on BIC courses, see here . About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Advanced Degree in the teaching or cognate discipline by the start date of the appointment. ABD or Master's considered. Teaching experience is preferred. Mission fit. All application materials must be submitted through Interfolio at the link found here:https://apply.interfolio.com/193551 A complication application consists of a cover letter, current curriculum vitae, and official transcript of the highest degree completed. If a Ph.D. is in progress, a transcript showing hours completed toward the Ph.D is also required. All applicants must complete the Part-Time Faculty Mission Review."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188134": {
      "title": "Lecturers, Part-time Temporary, Law School",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Waco, Texas Baylor Law School periodically seeks to appoint adjunct faculty to address instructional needs across a range of curricular areas.  Teaching opportunities for the upcoming academic year may include courses in labor law, creditor’s rights, franchising, and business succession planning.   Baylor Law places a high priority on in‑person instruction; accordingly, preference will be accorded to candidates who are able to conduct all or most of their class sessions on campus. Baylor law also periodically has needs for attorneys who can coach moot court, mock trial, transactional, and other competition teams.  These coaches are treated as adjunct faculty. The Law School welcomes expressions of interest in all areas of law and particularly encourages applications from all qualified candidates. As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and outstanding automatic retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you and your family's wellbeing and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. To learn more, go to Baylor Benefits & Advantages . To learn more about the School of Law, and Baylor University, please visit http://law.baylor.edu. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Juris Doctor Evidence of impressive academic performance in law school Practice experience in the area of interest Mission Fit Review of applicants will begin on June 30, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after May 31, 2027 can be considered. Candidates will be asked to provide the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Official Law School Transcript Names and contact information for three professional references In addition, all applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review form"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189876": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Tenure Track, Law, Trusts and Estates",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Waco, Texas Teaching responsibilities are based on Baylor Law's curricular needs and applicant's experience; however, the primary teaching responsibility is expected to include upper-level courses in trusts and estates, wealth transfers, estate planning, marital property, and estate administration.   Research responsibilities include developing and implementing a research agenda in the individual’s area of expertise. The Faculty Appointments Committee also encourages applicants whose expertise lies in other transactional practice areas. This appointment is a renewable full-time, benefits-eligible, tenure-track position as an Assistant Professor with the Baylor Law School with an anticipated appointment date of August 1, 2027. For more information, please visit the Baylor Law website: https://www.baylor.edu/law/facultystaff/index.php?id=980341 . About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Must possess an earned Juris Doctor Evidence of impressive academic performance in law school, such as a noteworthy ranking or GPA, earned law review membership and involvement, or other law school honors, (e.g., Order of the Coif membership). Experience as a practicing attorney in any field is preferred. Salary is commensurate with experience and qualifications. Review of applicants will begin on August 10, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after October 31, 2026 can be considered. Candidates will be asked to provide and/or complete the following: Cover Letter/Letter of Interest ( Candidates are encouraged to include a description of their interest in living and working in Central Texas) Curriculum vitae Law School Transcripts (Unofficial will be accepted but official will be required for on-campus interviews) Names and contact information for three professional references In addition, all applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form and Equal Employment Opportunity Form."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192460": {
      "title": "Lecturers, Part-time Temporary, Law School (Boot Camps)",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Law Location: Waco, Texas Baylor Law offers bootcamps to students at different times of the year that focus on practical skills training. Current bootcamps include those on Business Law, Criminal Law, Family Law, and Voir Dire. The lengths of the different bootcamps vary, with several of them taking place during a weekend. All bootcamp faculty are required to participate in person. Because of the nature of the bootcamps, preference will be accorded to candidates who have significant practical experience in the subject matter of the bootcamp(s) in which they are interested. As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and outstanding automatic retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you and your family's wellbeing and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. To learn more, go to Baylor Benefits & Advantages. To learn more about the School of Law, and Baylor University, please visit http://law.baylor.edu. About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Juris Doctor Evidence of impressive academic performance in law school Practice experience in the area of interest Mission Fit Review of applicants will begin on August 4, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after May 31, 2027 can be considered. Candidates will be asked to provide the following: Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Official Law School Transcript Names and contact information for three professional reference In addition, all applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review form"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185898": {
      "title": "Lecturers, Part-time (Adjunct) Temporary, Undergraduate: In Person and Online, Starting Fall 2026/Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Nursing Location: Dallas, Texas (Face to Face BSN Courses) and Online (Distance BSN courses) Position commences Fall (August 2026) & Spring (January 2027) Deadline For priority consideration, please submit a completed application by June 15th, 2026, for Fall 2026 employment and September 15th, 2026, for Spring 2027 employment. Position Type Part-time Faculty (Adjunct) - Multiple Hires Position URL hhttps://apply.interfolio.com/185898 Description Baylor University’s Louise Herrington School of Nursing is currently seeking candidates for part-time faculty in the Prelicensure face-to-face, hybrid and online tracks of our Bachelor of Science in Nursing program.  We are actively recruiting new faculty with a strong commitment to the classroom and an equally strong commitment to utilizing nursing knowledge as we fulfill our mission and pursue Baylor’s bold vision, Baylor in Deeds . The areas of responsibility will vary according to the faculty member's specialty area and specific assignment. About Baylor University and the Louise Herrington School of Nursing Located in Waco, Texas, Baylor University is the oldest college in Texas with a population of 21,000 diverse students. Baylor is one of the top universities in the nation, having been named an R1 institution by the Carnegie Commission on Higher Education. Baylor is also on the honor roll of the Great Colleges to Work For. Baylor has been offering nursing degrees in Dallas for more than a century. The Louise Herrington School of Nursing offers Bachelor of Science in Nursing and the Doctor of Nursing Practice degrees in our modern facility, conveniently located just east of downtown Dallas near the Baylor University Medical Center. Baylor University offers competitive salaries and benefits. A wide range of factors are considered in making compensation decisions, including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certification, analysis of internal equity, and other business and organizational needs. As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you and your family's well-being and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. The budgeted salary range that the University reasonably expects to pay for this position is $ 5,000.00 - $ 6,000.00 per 3-hour Didactic course. This range takes into account the wide range of factors that are considered in making compensation decisions, including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certification, analysis of internal equity, and other business and organizational needs. Each nurse faculty member shall: (A) hold a current license or privilege to practice as a registered nurse; (B) show evidence of teaching abilities and maintaining current knowledge, clinical expertise, and safety in subject area of teaching responsibility; (C) hold a master’s degree or doctorate degree, preferably in nursing; (D) a nurse faculty holding a master’s degree or doctorate degree in a discipline other than nursing shall hold a bachelor’s degree in nursing from an approved or accredited baccalaureate program in nursing; and shall have at least twelve (12) graduate semester hours in nursing appropriate to assigned teaching responsibilities. Bachelor’s and master’s degrees in nursing must be from an accredited institution. Minimum Experience Must have 3 years of teaching and/or nursing clinical experience within the last 5 years with evidence of current and recent full-time employment in either nursing education or clinical practice. Candidates should include a personal letter, a current curriculum vitae, transcripts, and one letter of recommendation. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio and complete the Baylor Questionnaire. Questions regarding the application process may be addressed to LHSON_Faculty_Apps@baylor.edu Please indicate your preferred term (Fall or Spring) and preferred teaching modality (face-to-face or online) Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Records to be uploaded to CastleBranch Tracker: Proof of HEP B immunization or immune titer Proof of negative TB test (including interpretation and induration) that will not expire before the end of the semester. Proof of Tdap immunization or immune titer Proof of MMR immunization or immune titer Proof of Varicella immunization or immune titer Proof of influenza vaccination (including manufacturer and lot number) Proof of Healthcare Provider CPR certification Proof of Medical Insurance (copy of policy face or coverage card.) Proof of RN licensure Copies of national board certifications Proof of COVID immunization"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/186106": {
      "title": "Lecturers, Part-time (Adjunct) Temporary, Graduate Nursing, Starting Fall 2026/Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Nursing Location: Dallas, Texas or Remote Baylor University’s Louise Herrington School of Nursing is currently seeking candidates for clinical track faculty positions to teach in the online Doctor of Nursing Practice program. We are actively recruiting new faculty with a strong commitment to the classroom and an equally strong commitment to utilizing nursing knowledge as we fulfill our mission and pursue Baylor’s bold vision, Baylor in Deeds .  The areas of teaching responsibility will vary according to the faculty member's specialty area and specific assignment. Position commences Fall 2026 (August) and Spring 2027 (January) This position may be on-site in Dallas or remote. Baylor is actively recruiting new faculty with a strong commitment to the online classroom and an equally strong commitment to building on our legacy of integrating faith and academic excellence in education. The LHSON supports Baylor University’s Academic Strategic Plan Baylor in Deeds . The nursing school serves as one of the pivotal anchors in the plan’s interdisciplinary areas of focus: Health, Data Sciences, Materials Science, Human Flourishing, Leadership & Ethics and Baylor in Latin America. Through impactful research and scholarship, the LHSON is giving light to new possibilities in nursing and other significant areas. Ultimately, Baylor aspires to become America's preeminent Christian research university. The Baylor University Louise Herrington School of Nursing (LHSON) is pleased to share the news of its placement in the U.S. News & World Report (USNWR) 2025 Best Graduate School rankings. In the U.S. News Best Nursing Schools rankings, the Doctor of Nursing Practice program in Baylor’s Louise Herrington School of Nursing in Dallas jumped into the Top 50 nationally at No. 43 . Baylor nursing’s DNP program offers eight tracks , including Family Nurse Practitioner, Nurse-Midwifery, Neonatal Nurse Practitioner, Pediatric Nurse Practitioner, Adult Gerontology Acute Care Nurse Practitioner, Executive Nurse Leadership and Psychiatric Mental Health Nurse Practitioner and the U.S. Army-Baylor Anesthesia Nursing (USAGPAN). As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and outstanding automatic retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you when your family’s wellbeing and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. To learn more go to Baylor Benefits and Advantages . The budgeted salary range that the University reasonably expects to pay for this position is $ 5,000.00 - $ 6,000.00 per 3-hour Didactic course This range takes into account the wide range of factors that are considered in making compensation decisions including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certification, analysis of internal equity, and other business and organizational needs. Board of Nursing Required Faculty Qualifications: Each nurse faculty member shall: (A) hold a current license or privilege to practice as a registered nurse and national certification as an advanced practice registered nurse; (B) show evidence of teaching abilities and maintaining current knowledge, clinical expertise, and safety in subject area of teaching responsibility; (C) hold a MSN or DNP/PhD; (D) Nursing degrees must be from accredited institutions. (E) National board certification as an APRN. Minimum Experience: Must have 2 years of teaching and/or full-time APRN clinical experience within the last 5 years. Candidates should submit a personal letter, a current curriculum vitae, official transcripts, and one letter of recommendation. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio. Background and Drug Screens are required and applicants will be contacted regarding screening procedures and enrollment in Castlebranch Tracker.  The tracker is an online system for tracking documents and records required for clinical affiliation. For priority consideration, please submit a completed application by June 15th, 2026, for Fall 2026 employment and September 15th, 2026, for Spring 2027 employment. Records to be uploaded to CastleBranch Tracker: Proof of HEP B immunization or immune titer Proof of negative TB test (including interpretation and induration) that will not expire before the end of the semester. Proof of Tdap immunization or immune titer Proof of MMR immunization or immune titer Proof of Varicella immunization or immune titer Proof of influenza vaccination (including manufacturer and lot number) Proof of Healthcare Provider CPR certification Proof of Medical Insurance (copy of policy face or coverage card.) Proof of RN licensure Copies of national board certifications"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/189365": {
      "title": "Clinical Professor (all ranks), Undergraduate Nursing, Starting Spring 2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Nursing Location: Dallas, Texas Baylor University's Louise Herrington School of Nursing seeks candidates for a clinical track faculty position teaching face-to-face in the Bachelor of Science in Nursing program. We are actively recruiting new faculty with a strong commitment to the classroom and an equally strong commitment to utilizing nursing knowledge as we fulfill our mission and pursue Baylor's bold vision, Baylor in Deeds . Responsibilities include teaching and related scholarly work, clinical scholarship, and service to the school and the university, the faculty, the professional field, the religious congregation, and the community. The areas of responsibility will vary according to the faculty member's specialty area and specific assignment. Preferred Specialties : Psych Mental Health Baylor University offers competitive salaries and benefits. The budgeted salary range that the university expects to pay for this position is $80,000 to $120,000. A wide range of factors are considered in making compensation decisions, including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certification, analysis of internal equity, and other business and organizational needs. As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you and your family's well-being and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. About Baylor University in Dallas : As the oldest college in Texas, Baylor University has a population of 21,000 diverse students. While the main campus is in Waco, Texas , Baylor has been offering nursing degrees in Dallas for over a century. Currently, Baylor University is offering nursing courses in our modern facility conveniently located in downtown Dallas by the Baylor University Medical Center. Baylor is one of the top universities in the nation, having been named an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification in January 2022. Baylor is also on the honor roll of the \"Great Colleges to Work For\" from The Chronicle of Higher Education; Baylor offers competitive salaries and benefits while giving faculty and staff a chance to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. Our strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as we continue to live up to Baylor's mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Qualified applicants for clinical track appointments must possess an earned doctoral degree (or have completed at least 75% of the degree) and national certification in a clinical specialty. All candidates must hold an unencumbered license as a Registered Nurse in Texas. Must be able to work in person at the Dallas academic building. Please note that this is not an online position. Candidates should include: a cover letter/letter of application, a current curriculum vitae, transcripts of the highest degree earned or in progress, three confidential letters of recommendation. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form (RAF) and the Baylor School of Nursing Questionnaire in Interfolio. Questions regarding the application process may be addressed to LHSON_Faculty_Apps@baylor.edu Deadline Please submit a completed application by September 30th, 2026, for priority consideration. Position URL - apply.interfolio.com/189365"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194709": {
      "title": "Clinical Professor (all ranks), Graduate Nursing, Starting Spring 2027/Fall 2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Nursing Location: Dallas, Texas. This position may be in Dallas and/or remote. Description Baylor University's Louise Herrington School of Nursing is currently seeking candidates for clinical track faculty positions teaching in the online Doctor of Nursing Practice program. We are actively recruiting new faculty with a strong commitment to the classroom and an equally strong commitment to utilizing nursing knowledge as we fulfill our mission and pursue Baylor's bold vision, Baylor in Deeds . Responsibilities include teaching and related scholarly work, clinical scholarship, and service to the school and the university, the faculty member's professional field, one's religious congregation, and the community. The areas of responsibility will vary according to the faculty member's specialty area and specific assignment. Preferred Specialties : Family Nurse Practitioner and Psych Mental Health Baylor University offers competitive salaries and benefits. The budgeted salary range that the university expects to pay for this position is $80,000 to $120,000. A wide range of factors are considered in making compensation decisions, including but not limited to experience, skills, knowledge, abilities, education, licensure and certification, analysis of internal equity, and other business and organizational needs. As part of the Baylor family, eligible employees receive a comprehensive benefits package that includes medical, dental, and vision insurance, generous time off, tuition remission, and retirement contributions. To the extent that a faculty or staff member is benefits eligible, Baylor has a comprehensive benefits plan that supports you and your family's well-being and allows you to be a part of the life of a vibrant and active college campus. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. About Baylor University in Dallas : As the oldest college in Texas, Baylor University has a population of 21,000 diverse students. While the main campus is in Waco, Texas , Baylor has been offering nursing degrees in Dallas for over a century. Currently, Baylor University is offering nursing courses in our modern facility conveniently located in downtown Dallas by the Baylor University Medical Center. Baylor is one of the top universities in the nation, having been named an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification in January 2022. Baylor is also on the honor roll of the \"Great Colleges to Work For\" from The Chronicle of Higher Education; Baylor offers competitive salaries and benefits while giving faculty and staff a chance to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. Our strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as we continue to live up to Baylor's mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Qualified applicants for clinical track appointments must possess an earned doctoral degree (or have completed at least 75% of the degree) and national certification in a clinical specialty. All candidates must hold an unencumbered license as a Registered Nurse in the state of Texas or their state of residence. Candidates should include: a cover letter/letter of applicaiton, a current curriculum vitae, official transcripts of the highest degree earned or in progress, three confidential letters of recommendation. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form (RAF) and the Baylor School of Nursing Questionnaire in Interfolio.  Questions regarding the application process may be addressed to LHSON_Faculty_Apps@baylor.edu Deadline Please submit a completed application by Nov. 10th, 2026, for Spring 2027 priority consideration. Review of Spring 2027 applications will begin Nov. 10th and continue until the position is filled. Please submit a completed application by Jan. 4th, 2027, for Fall 2027 priority consideration. Review of Fall 2027 applications will begin Jan. 4th and continue until the position is filled. Nothing received after March 31st, 2027 can be considered. Location Online Instruction; residence in Dallas Metroplex preferred. https://apply.interfolio.com/194709"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/183599": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Part-Time, Temporary A/Y 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Seminary Location: Waco, Texas This position is listed for Part Time lecturer positions for Truett Seminary.  These positions will teach courses for Truett Seminary at either the Waco, Houston, or San Antonio campuses.  Required documents to be included with application are CV and official transcript of terminal degree. Doctoral degree or the terminal degree required. Apply through Interfolio. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio as well as a cover letter, CV, and official transcript of the terminal degree."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187109": {
      "title": "Spiritual Formation Program Covenant Group Mentor: Lecturer, Part-Time, Temporary A/Y 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Seminary Location: Waco, Houston, San Antonio The covenant group mentor serves a vital role in accomplishing the learning outcomes of Truett’s Spiritual Formation program by shepherding students, teaching practical wisdom, asking good questions, and modeling Christ-like character. At the conclusion of the spiritual formation program, covenant group mentors help students to: Articulate a basic understanding of the processes and goals of spiritual formation and discipleship in relationship to God's story and one's own story. Demonstrate a commitment to a life of intentional spiritual development and accountability to spiritual companions and mentors. Provide mutual encouragement and support for a diverse group of individuals by listening and responding with empathy and spiritual discernment in small groups. Exhibit a commitment to self-awareness and self-assessment as a disciple of Christ who is being formed for service to God in the world. Integrate disciplines of prayer and other forms of practice into daily life and describe the implications for personal spiritual formation. Develop a personal covenant in collaboration with others close to them in order to discern a path of vocation and devotion in their relationship with God. To accomplish these outcomes, the covenant group mentor will: Work in collaboration with course instructors to teach and disciple students. Be present for large group gathering with instructors and small group gatherings with their covenant group. Focus students on the given lesson aims for each class session. Facilitate a conversation among students, fostering constructive dialogue. Communicate with the Spiritual Formation office when the mentor anticipates being absent, and to appoint a student to guide discussion and take attendance on the date of absence. Record attendance using designated online tools. Read student reflections to prepare for discussions. Remain compliant with Baylor University’s policies and procedures (completion of Title IX training, etc.). Lead groups in a spirit that is consistent with Truett Seminary’s “ Vision for Common Life Together ” and “ Statement of Common Faith .” Pray for students, instructors, and Baylor's Truett Seminary, that our work may be done unto God and for God's glory. Masters level degree, specialized knowledge in the area of spiritual formation or spiritual direction, and/or significant ministry experience. Apply through Interfolio.  All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio. All applicants must submit an updated CV and Transcript of the highest degree earned in the field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/187110": {
      "title": "Spiritual Direction Training Program Supervisor: Lecturer, Part-Time, Temporary A/Y 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Seminary Location: Waco The Spiritual Direction Training Program Supervisor assists in the training of Certified Spiritual Directors by taking part in class discussion, assessing student work, and guiding a spiritual direction supervision group through the curriculum. Masters level degree, specialized knowledge in the area of spiritual formation and spiritual direction, and significant practical ministry experience. Certification as a spiritual director required; certification as a spiritual direction supervisor preferred. Apply through Interfolio.  All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio. All applicants must submit an updated CV and Transcript of the highest degree earned in the field."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188402": {
      "title": "Part-Time TIP Gifted Program Instructor (Super Saturday - Waco/Dallas Locations), Baylor TIP",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Education: EDU Buffer: School of Education Dean and Administrative Location: Waco, Texas Part-Time TIP Gifted Program Instructors for Baylor TIP provide teaching and mentoring experiences to the students who qualify for and participate in the talent development programs offered through The Center for Gifted Education and Talent Development. These programs include but are not limited to Super Saturdays, summer commuter classes, academic clubs and societies, academic competitions, and University for Young People. TIP Gifted Program Instructors may provide both online and on-the-ground instruction. Job Responsibilities · Complete all required trainings and job-specific assignments prior to working with students, including submitting lesson plans and supply requests by given deadlines · Engage students in the content by utilizing best educational practices · Assist students in identifying and understanding their talents and college/career goals · Actively supervise students to ensure their safety and well-being · Adhere to and enforce all safety and risk management policies · Collaborate with Center leadership team to resolve any student disciplinary issues or concerns in an appropriate manner that aligns with the Center’s mission and policies · Maintain positive relationships with students, parents, and staff · Complete all tasks required for effectively closing the assignment (submitting student evaluations, returning supplies that belong to Baylor, reporting any property damage that occurred during the activity, etc.) About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as one of the top universities in the nation, achieving R1 institution status by the Carnegie Classification in January 2022. Baylor also made it to the honor roll of \"Great Colleges to Work For\" from The Chronicle of Higher Education . It offers competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Minimum Qualifications · Bachelor’s degree · Content expertise in the area of instruction (e.g., mathematics, engineering etc.) · Pedagogical skills and/or previous experience teaching children or adolescents · Ability to communicate effectively and positively with students, families, and other staff · Strong organizational skills Preferred Qualifications · Completed graduate degree in content area being taught. Relevant professional experience in content area being taught may substitute for graduate-level coursework. · Teaching certificate with specific GT training if an Instructor of students in grades 1-7 · Experience in instructional design · Teaching experience with high ability students · Experience providing exceptional customer service Candidates should include the following in their application materials: (a) a cover letter, (b) completed application form, (c) a curriculum vitae, (d) transcripts documenting the terminal degree, and (e) contact information for 3 professional references. Additional documents, such as teaching certificates and other applicable materials may also be included. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Part-Time Mission Review in Interfolio."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192243": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Non-Tenure Track, School of Music, Music Theory",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Music: MUS Buffer: Music Academic and Professional Studies Location: Waco, TX The School of Music at Baylor University seeks an engaging and collaborative educator who excels at teaching undergraduate music theory classes. This full-time, non-tenure track, Assistant Teaching Professor appointment will begin in August 2027. About the Music Theory Area : With six full-time music theorists, the School of Music’s theory area is committed to delivering a robust core curriculum that serves all our majors. Through scholarship and specialized courses in theory and analysis, we partner with other divisions within the School to prepare our students with a solid foundation that serves them well as music professionals. The theory area is part of the Academic Studies Division, which provides support for individual and collaborative research as well as opportunities for sharing and developing ideas related to pedagogy. About the Baylor School of Music : At the Baylor School of Music, faith and artistry meet purpose. Our faculty guide exceptional students through a rigorous, musically rich education, where every performance, composition, and collaboration reflects excellence and depth. From captivating performers to musical scholars to contemporary innovators, Baylor shapes versatile artists and educators who thrive creatively and professionally. What sets the Baylor School of Music apart? A vibrant community of 300 students from across the country and around the globe. Award-winning ensembles that have performed at every major national conference in the United States, and many of the world’s most prestigious venues. Our Emmy-nominated television concert event, A Baylor Christmas, reaches 150,000 households during the holidays. Over 300 live performances annually, giving students real-world experiences that prepare them for professional careers. Global opportunities through study abroad and mission trips—from Kenya to Japan to Brazil. Winners of Semper Pro Musica, our signature student competition, perform at Carnegie Hall. Research opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. Baylor’s mission to integrate academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Joining the Baylor School of Music means becoming part of a legacy that combines rigorous artistry and academic pursuits with a deep sense of purpose. Here, we prepare a new generation of musicians and scholars to lead, serve, and inspire. Responsibilities and Expectations: Teach courses with in the undergraduate theory and ear-training curriculum (Note: Teaching composition is not part of the job re sponsibilities .) Teach other courses, as needed Participate in School of Music and University service as appropriate Contribute to the intellectual life and mission of Baylor University and the School of Music Appointment Date: August 15, 2027 Rank: Appointment at Assistant Teaching Professor, full-time, non-tenure track, promotion eligible Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Terminal degree in m usic t heory or composition completed by the appointment date Successful record of teaching music theory as instructor of record at the collegiate level Keyboard skills All School of Music faculty are expected to demonstrate commitment to creating a caring and supportive learning environment for our students Applicants shall submit the following materials: Signed letter of application Curriculum Vitae Official transcript of highest academic degree earned Complete contact information (phone and email) for three to five professional references All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form in Interfolio Additional materials (e.g. , letters of recommendation, teaching video s ) may be requested at a later date Active r eview of applications will begin on October 15 , 202 6 and will continue until the position is filled. Preference may be given to those who apply by October 15 th . Following initial revie w , applicants who advance in the search process may be asked to send additional materials. Materials received after December 15, 2026 cannot be considered. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Please direct inquir i es to: Dr. Horace J. Maxile Professor of Music Theory Chair of Music Theory Search Committee horace_maxile@baylor.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192307": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Tenure-Track, Music, Music Theory",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Music: MUS Buffer: Music Academic and Professional Studies Location: Waco, TX The School of Music at Baylor University seeks an engaging scholar and collaborative educator who excels in research and teaching (undergraduate and graduate). This full-time, tenure track, Assistant or Associate Professor appointment will begin in August 2027. About the Music Theory Area : With six full-time music theorists, the School of Music’s theory area is committed to delivering a robust core curriculum that serves all our majors. Through scholarship and specialized courses in theory and analysis, we partner with other divisions within the School to prepare our students with a solid foundation that serves them well as music professionals. The theory area is part of the Academic Studies Division, which provides support for individual and collaborative research as well as opportunities for sharing and developing ideas related to pedagogy. About the Baylor School of Music : At the Baylor School of Music, faith and artistry meet purpose. Our faculty guide exceptional students through a rigorous, musically rich education, where every performance, composition, and collaboration reflects excellence and depth. From captivating performers to musical scholars to contemporary innovators, Baylor shapes versatile artists and educators who thrive creatively and professionally. What sets the Baylor School of Music apart? A vibrant community of 300 students from across the country and around the globe. Award-winning ensembles that have performed at every major national conference in the United States, and many of the world’s most prestigious venues. Our Emmy-nominated television concert event, A Baylor Christmas, reaches 150,000 households during the holidays. Over 300 live performances annually, giving students real-world experiences that prepare them for professional careers. Global opportunities through study abroad and mission trips—from Kenya to Japan to Brazil. Winners of Semper Pro Musica, our signature student competition, perform at Carnegie Hall. Research opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. Baylor’s mission to integrate academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Joining the Baylor School of Music means becoming part of a legacy that combines rigorous artistry and academic pursuits with a deep sense of purpose. Here, we prepare a new generation of musicians and scholars to lead, serve, and inspire. Responsibilities and Expectations : Maintain an active program of research and publication in music theory appropriate for faculty at a major research university Teach courses within the undergraduate theory and ear-training curriculum and graduate courses, as needed Participate in School of Music and University service as appropriate Contribute to the intellectual life and mission of Baylor University and the School of Music Appointment Date: August 15, 202 7 Rank: Ass istant /Ass ociate Professor, full-time, tenure - track Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience About Baylor University Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. PhD in Music Theory ( or the equivalent ) completed by the appointment date Successful record of teaching music theory as instructor of record at the collegiate level Record of and/or potential for achievement in the field at a national or international level All School of Music faculty are expected to demonstrate commitment to creating a caring and supportive learning environment for our students Applicants shall submit the following materials: Signed letter of application Curriculum Vitae Official transcript of highest academic degree earned Complete contact information (phone and email) for three to five professional references All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form in Interfolio Additional materials (e.g. , letters of recommendation, teaching video s ) may be requested at a later date. Active r eview of applications will begin on October 1 , 202 6 and will continue until the position is filled. Preference may be given to those who apply by October 1, 2026. Following initial revie w , applicants who advance in the search process may be asked to send additional materials. Materials submitted after December 1, 2026 will not be considered. Please note that the Baylor School of Music is also hiring an Assistant Teaching Professor in Music Theory. Candidates applying for the tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant /Associate Professor are invited to apply for the Assistant Teaching Professor in Music Theory position , as well. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Horace J. Maxile Professor of Music Theory Chair of Music Theory Search Committee horace_maxile@baylor.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192407": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor, Tenure-Track, Music, Musicology",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Music: MUS Buffer: Music Academic and Professional Studies Location: Waco, TX The School of Music at Baylor University seeks a nationally recognized musicologist or ethnomusicologist whose active and impactful research agenda is complemented by a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students. This full-time, tenure-track, Assistant or Associate Professor appointment will begin on August 15, 2027. About the Musicology Area: Baylor University’s Musicology Area offers an inspiring blend of rigorous scholarship, personalized mentorship, and vibrant musical engagement. Faculty are active in national and international discourse and engage in research across a wide range of historical and cultural specialties, including global music history, American music, ethnomusicology, Latin American music, and performance practice. Students explore the rich traditions and cultural contexts of music while developing exceptional skills in critical thinking, research, and writing. With small undergraduate class sizes, dedicated faculty specialists, and interdisciplinary opportunities throughout a major research university, Baylor provides an ideal environment for emerging scholars. Graduate students benefit from the program’s structure, characterized by small cohorts and individualized attention. Students are supported by a program known for its professional training, humanistic perspective, and commitment to student success, and they go on to thrive in diverse careers, from arts administration to teaching, community outreach, editing, performance, and research. About the Baylor School of Music: At the Baylor School of Music, faith and artistry meet purpose. Our faculty guide exceptional students through a rigorous, musically rich education, where every performance, composition, and collaboration reflects excellence and depth. From captivating performers to musical scholars to contemporary innovators, Baylor shapes versatile artists and educators who thrive creatively and professionally. What sets the Baylor School of Music apart? A vibrant community of 300 students from across the country and around the globe. Award-winning ensembles that have performed at every major national conference in the United States, and many of the world’s most prestigious venues. Our Emmy-nominated television concert event, A Baylor Christmas, reaches 150,000 households during the holidays. Over 300 live performances annually, giving students real-world experiences that prepare them for professional careers. Global opportunities through study abroad and mission trips—from Kenya to Japan to Brazil. Winners of Semper Pro Musica, our signature student competition, perform at Carnegie Hall. Research opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. Baylor’s mission to integrate academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Joining the Baylor School of Music means becoming part of a legacy that combines rigorous artistry with a deep sense of purpose. Here, students, faculty, and staff together prepare a new generation of musicians and scholars to lead, serve, and inspire. Responsibilities and Expectations: Teach core undergraduate music culture and history courses, as well as graduate seminars, topics in the scholar's particular field of expertise, and other courses as assigned Maintain an active agenda of research and publication Collaborate closely with musicology and ethnomusicology faculty Demonstrate a commitment to fostering a caring and supportive learning environment through teaching, mentorship, and engagement in student scholarly and artistic activities Participate in Area, Division, Department, School of Music, and University service and activities Engage in significant research and/or professional and creative activities within the discipline and contribute to the intellectual life and Christian mission of Baylor University and the School of Music Appointment Date: August 15, 2027 Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor, full-time, tenure-track Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience About Baylor University Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required: PhD in Musicology , Ethnomusicology , or related field at time of appointment (August 15, 2027) Evidence of successful c lassroom t eaching at the collegiate level Evidence of strong scholarly productivity and/or potential Experience teaching both music majors and non-majors Applicants shall submit the following materials electronically : Signed letter of application Curriculum v itae O fficial transcript of highest academic degree earned Teaching p hilosophy s tatement Two examples of scholarly writing Contact information (address, phone, and email) for three professional references. Completed self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form C andidates moving forward in the interview process may be asked to provide three recommendation letters, as well as documented evidence of teaching effectiveness ( e.g. sample syllabi , sample assignments, student evaluations, links to videos of teaching, etc.) . Active r eview of applications will begin on October 12 , 2026, a nd will continue until the position is filled. Following initial revie w , applicants who advance in the search process may be asked to send additional materials. Materials received after December 15, 2026, will not be considered. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Alfredo Colman Associate Professor of Musicology/Ethnomusicology Chair of the Musicology Search Committee Alfredo_Colman@baylor.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193051": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Church Music and Director, Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies, Tenure-Track, Music, Church Music",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Music: MUS Buffer: Music Academic and Professional Studies Location: Waco, TX The School of Music at Baylor University seeks an engaging and collaborative academic leader to serve as Assistant or Associate Professor of Church Music and Director of the Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies. The successful candidate will provide visionary leadership for the Dunn Center, engage in influential scholarly work, contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in church music, and cultivate meaningful connections among the academy, the church, and the broader field of Christian music and worship. About Church Music and the Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies Church Music at Baylor brings together rigorous musical study, theological reflection, research, and practical formation for service to the church. Students pursue undergraduate and graduate pathways in Church Music, including master’s and doctoral study and a joint M.Div./M.M. with George W. Truett Theological Seminary. The Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies extends this work through research initiatives, conferences and public programs, visiting artists and scholars, church and professional partnerships, and global Music & Missions opportunities. Faculty and staff work collaboratively to prepare musicians and scholars for thoughtful, creative, and faithful leadership in a wide range of Christian denominations and communities. About the Baylor School of Music : At the Baylor School of Music, faith and artistry meet purpose. Our faculty guide exceptional students through a rigorous, musically rich education, where every performance, composition, and collaboration reflects excellence and depth. From captivating performers to musical scholars to contemporary innovators, Baylor shapes versatile artists and educators who thrive creatively and professionally. What sets the Baylor School of Music apart? A vibrant community of 300 students from across the country and around the globe. Award-winning ensembles that have performed at every major national conference in the United States, and many of the world’s most prestigious venues. Our Emmy-nominated television concert event, A Baylor Christmas, reaches 150,000 households during the holidays. Over 300 live performances annually, giving students real-world experiences that prepare them for professional careers. Global opportunities through study abroad and mission trips—from Kenya to Japan to Brazil. Winners of Semper Pro Musica, our signature student competition, perform at Carnegie Hall. Research opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. Baylor’s mission to integrate academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Joining the Baylor School of Music means becoming part of a legacy that combines rigorous artistry and academic pursuits with a deep sense of purpose. Here, we prepare a new generation of musicians and scholars to lead, serve, and inspire. Responsibilities : Provide strategic and administrative leadership for the Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies, including its programs, research initiatives, public engagement, partnerships, staff, and financial resources. Work collaboratively with Church Music faculty and Dunn Center staff to sustain and develop programs that serve Baylor students, scholars, church musicians, congregations, and the broader field. Cultivate relationships with churches, professional organizations, alumni, donors, academic partners, and other Baylor units, including George W. Truett Theological Seminary, as appropriate to the Center’s mission. Maintain an active scholarly, creative, and/or professional agenda with visibility and contribution appropriate to a research university. Participate in teaching courses in Church Music/Worship in areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and program needs and supervise graduate research as appropriate. Participate in School of Music and University service as appropriate Appointment Date: July 1, 202 7 Rank: Ass istant or A ss ociate Professor, full-time, tenure - track Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience About Baylor University Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits, allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination, Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds, guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required An earned doctoral or terminal degree in Church Music, Worship Studies, Sacred Music, Music, Theology/Religion, or other closely related field. Demonstrated leadership or administrative experience relevant to an academic center/program, church or ministry organization, nonprofit, or comparable setting. Substantial knowledge of Christian worship and church music, with the ability to connect scholarship, musical practice, ministry, and the practical needs of churches. A record of significant scholarly, creative, and/or professional activity appropriate to appointment at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentoring, with preparation to teach Church Music/Worship at the undergraduate and graduate levels. Demonstrated commitment to creating an inclusive learning environment. Preferred Experience leading a center, academic program, or major initiative, requiring strategic planning and project execution. Experience supervising staff, managing budgets, and/or working with advisory boards or external partners. Demonstrated success with grants, external funding, donor relationships, or other forms of resource development. A record of national and/or international professional engagement and partnership-building. Experience advising graduate research, theses, dissertations, or doctoral projects. Applicants shall submit the following materials: Signed letter of application addressing interest in the position and relevant preparation. Curriculum Vitae, including scholarly/creative/professional accomplishments and evidence of teaching/mentoring where applicable. Official transcript of the highest academic degree earned. Complete contact information (address, phone, and email) for three professional references. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form in Interfolio. Active review of applications will begin on October 26, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Preference may be given to those who apply by the review date. Following initial review, applicants who advance in the search process may be asked to send additional materials. Materials received after January 25, 2027 will not be considered. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Monique M. Ingalls Chair, Church Music Search Committee Professor of Music and Ammann Endowed Chair in Church Music and Worship Chair, Academic & Professional Studies Department Monique_Ingalls@baylor.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193052": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor, Non-Tenure Track, School of Music, Church Music",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Music: MUS Buffer: Music Academic and Professional Studies Location: Waco, TX The School of Music at Baylor University seeks an engaging and collaborative church musician and educator to join its Church Music faculty as a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor. The successful candidate will contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching, student mentoring and recruitment, and the School of Music’s ongoing work of preparing skilled and thoughtful musicians for service in churches and other ministry contexts. About Church Music and the Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies : Church Music at Baylor brings together rigorous musical study, theological reflection, research, and practical formation for service to the church. Students pursue undergraduate and graduate pathways in Church Music, including master’s and doctoral study and a joint M.Div./M.M. with George W. Truett Theological Seminary. The Dunn Center for Christian Music Studies extends this work through research initiatives, conferences and public programs, visiting artists and scholars, church and professional partnerships, and global Music & Missions opportunities. Faculty and staff work collaboratively to prepare musicians and scholars for thoughtful, creative, and faithful leadership in a wide range of Christian denominations and communities. About the Baylor School of Music : At the Baylor School of Music, faith and artistry meet purpose. Our faculty guide exceptional students through a rigorous, musically rich education, where every performance, composition, and collaboration reflects excellence and depth. From captivating performers to musical scholars to contemporary innovators, Baylor shapes versatile artists and educators who thrive creatively and professionally. What sets the Baylor School of Music apart? A vibrant community of 300 students from across the country and around the globe. Award-winning ensembles that have performed at every major national conference in the United States, and many of the world’s most prestigious venues. Our Emmy-nominated television concert event, A Baylor Christmas, reaches 150,000 households during the holidays. Over 300 live performances annually, giving students real-world experiences that prepare them for professional careers. Global opportunities through study abroad and mission trips—from Kenya to Japan to Brazil. Winners of Semper Pro Musica, our signature student competition, perform at Carnegie Hall. Research opportunities for graduate and undergraduate students. Baylor’s mission to integrate academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Joining the Baylor School of Music means becoming part of a legacy that combines rigorous artistry and academic pursuits with a deep sense of purpose. Here, we prepare a new generation of musicians and scholars to lead, serve, and inspire. Responsibilities: Teach undergraduate and, as appropriate, graduate courses in Church Music/Worship, with particular attention to practical ministry, vocational formation, and areas aligned with the candidate’s expertise and program needs. Mentor students preparing for professional ministry and church-music vocations, helping them integrate musical excellence, theological reflection, pastoral awareness, and practical leadership. Support internships, field-based learning, ministry placements, or other experiential learning as assigned. Participate actively in recruitment, auditions/admissions activities, student advising, and retention efforts for Church Music programs. Collaborate with Church Music faculty and Dunn Center staff on events, educational programs, outreach, and partnerships with churches and ministry leaders. Maintain active professional and/or creative engagement in church music, worship, ministry, or related fields. Participate in School of Music and University service as appropriate. Engage in significant professional and creative activities and/or research within the discipline and contribute to the intellectual life and mission of Baylor University and the School of Music. Appointment Date: August 15, 2027 Rank: Appointment at Assistant Teaching Professor, full-time, non-tenure track, promotion eligible Salary: Competitive and commensurate with experience About Baylor University: Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as an R1 institution by the Carnegie Classification. Baylor is also noted on the honor roll of “Great Colleges to Work For” by ModernThink. We offer competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. As a Christian university with historic Baptist roots and as reflected in the Notice of Non-Discrimination , Baylor strives to be a place characterized by civility and respect. We believe each student, faculty, staff, and administrator is made in the image of God. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Required A master’s degree in Church Music, Worship Studies, Sacred Music, Music, Ministry/Theology, or a closely related field. Substantial professional experience in church music, worship leadership, music ministry, or a closely related form of Christian ministry practice. Evidence of effective teaching in collegiate, seminary, church-based, or comparable educational settings. Broad practical knowledge of music and worship ministry, with the ability to connect musical, theological, pastoral, organizational, and vocational dimensions of practice. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively and to mentor students preparing for varied forms of service in churches and ministry settings. Demonstrated commitment to creating an inclusive learning environment. Preferred A doctoral degree in a relevant field or equivalent distinguished professional experience. College-level teaching experience and the ability to teach across multiple practical and academic areas within Church Music/Worship. Experience with field education, internships, ministry formation, curricular development, or supervision of student practitioners. Experience in student recruitment, admissions, advising, or retention. Active professional networks among churches, worship leaders, church musicians, and/or professional organizations across a range of Christian traditions Applicants shall submit the following materials: Signed letter of application addressing interest in the position and relevant preparation Curriculum Vitae, including professional/ministry experience and evidence of teaching/mentoring where applicable. Official transcript of the highest academic degree earned. Complete contact information (address, phone, and email) for three professional references. All applicants must complete the self-disclosed Religious Affiliation Form in Interfolio. Active review of applications will begin on October 12, 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Preference may be given to those who apply by the review date.  Following initial review, applicants who advance in the search process may be asked to send additional materials. Materials received after January 12, 2027 will not be considered. Candidates who are outside of the United States and do not have other nonimmigrant visa status will not be sponsored for an H-1B petition. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Monique M. Ingalls Ammann Endowed Chair in Church Music and Worship Chair, Academic & Professional Studies Department Chair, Church Music Search Committee Monique_Ingalls@baylor.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/184669": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Part-Time Temporary, Physician Assistant Department, 2026-27",
      "description": "Department: Baylor University: Office of the Provost: Health and Human Sciences: HHS Buffer: Physician Assistant Studies Location: Waco, TX and/or Temple, TX  Adjunct Faculty Role Adjunct Faculty are essential for curricular delivery in the Baylor Physician Assistant Program . Their contributions are most prominent during immersive learning experiences held on the Baylor campus in Waco, Texas , though instruction may also occur through synchronous or asynchronous teaching methods . A djunct Faculty bring essential knowledge, skills, and contemporary professional expertise that enrich students’ learning and development . They demonstrate teaching proficiency and a commitment to student success by delivering assigned course content in a way that promotes meaningful learning and mastery of course material. Primary Functions Teaching is the primary responsibility of Adjunct Faculty member s . Responsibilities may include some or all of the following , based on the terms and conditions of their appointment: Deliver assigned courses and complete associated tasks in accordance with the Program’s approved course description(s) and competency-based student learning outcomes and instructional objectives . Meet deadlines and follow directions of the lead course instructor(s), p rogram and Department leadership, and the Dean or Associate Dean of the College. Demonstrate expertise in the discipline and apply effective instructional delivery methods . Maintain accurate and complete records of student participation and performance. U tilize appropriate methods to assess student performance in alignment with established learning outcomes , and p rovide timely , constructive feedback . Submit grades and other required documentation in a timely manner . Collaborate with the appropriate principal f aculty members , the Chair of Student Success and Progression , and the Program Director to identify and address student issues as appropriate . Collaborate with the appropriate prin ci pal f aculty members and the Program Director to support student progress and address academic or behavioral concerns . Adhere to professional and ethical standards of conduct in all instructional and professional interactions . Maintain current certification and state medical licensure . Promote professionalism by modeling and reinforcing professional standards/ behaviors in academic and clinical setting s . Assist with course - related activities and fulfill other duties as assigned by the lead course instructor(s) . Review and acknowledge receipt of the Post-Course Instructional Faculty Performance Evaluation within the time period designated by the Academic Program Manager. Secondary Functions These expectations do not apply to all adjunct faculty positions and will vary based on appointment terms : Plan and deliver course content through face -to- face lab instruction , adhering to the syllabus , guidance from lead course instructor(s), Program leadership , and other University requirements. Participate in course, curriculum, and program -level assessment activities. Attend and conduct all scheduled classes , including exams and any program-related assessments, in accordance with the academic calendar . Compensation and Benefits The budgeted compensation for this position is $75.00 per lab contact hour. This rate reflects a variety of considerations , including experience, educational background, professional skills, knowledge, and abilities, as well as licensure and certification s. Compensation decisions are also informed by internal equity reviews , market benchmarks, and broader organizational priorities and budgetary factors . About Baylor University Baylor University is located in Waco, Texas and is the oldest college in the state. It has a diverse student population of 21,000 and is recognized as one of the top universities in the nation, achieving R1 institution status by the Carnegie Classification in January 2022. Baylor also made it to the honor roll of \"Great Colleges to Work For\" from The Chronicle of Higher Education . It offers competitive salaries and benefits , allowing faculty and staff to live in one of the fastest-growing parts of the state. Baylor's new strategic plan, Baylor in Deeds , guides the University as it continues to fulfill its mission of educating men and women for worldwide leadership and service by integrating academic excellence and Christian commitment within a caring community. Minimum Qualifications : License: Physician assistant with an active NCCPA certification and state license, physician with an active specialty board certification and state license, or nurse practitioner (or equivalent) with an active certification and state license, or other healthcare instructor with appropriate licensure and certification (if applicable) in instruction. Education: Graduate of an ARC-PA accredited PA Program, MD/DO degree from an accredited university, or advanced nursing degree from an accredited university and program, or for other healthcare instructors, appropriate academic preparation, degree, or formal training relevant to the area of instruction. Clinical Experience: Physician assistants or physicians are required to have clinical experience in a medical field directly relevant to the content taught. Advanced nurses are required to have 2 years of clinical experience in a medical field directly relevant to the content taught. Other healthcare instructors are required to have 2 years of clinical experience in the area of instruction. Content Expertise: Direct clinical knowledge and experience with the skills and knowledge required to be delivered. Preferred Qualifications : Minimum t wo ( 2 ) years of experience in physician assistant education . Availability to participate in immersive sessions requiring at least three ( 3 ) consecutive days of on-site presence, including travel if applicable . 3 years of clinical experience in a medical field directly relevant to the content taught. Additional Skills: Ability to effectively teach hands-on medical skills and procedures to small and large student groups with minimal oversight . Exhibited evidence of continued professional growth and development Demonstrated commitment to the use of innovative instructional techniques that enhance engagement and learning outcomes. Areas of Current Skill and Experience Need ed : 1. Comprehensive history taking 2. Comprehensive physical examinations 3. Human donor dissection 4. OSCE grading 5. Local and digital anesthesia 6. Toenail removal and nail trephination 7. Abscess incision and drainage 8. Ear and nose foreign body removal 9. Ring and fishhook removal 10. Naso- and orogastric tube placement 11. Intraosseous access 12. Knee and shoulder arthrocentesis 13. Adult airway management 14. Pediatric airway management 15. Skin punch, shave, elliptical biopsy 16. Skin closure 17. Suture hand ties 18. Peripheral IV placement and blood draws 19. Lumbar punctures 20. Needle, finger, tubal chest thoracostomy 21. Cricothyroidotomy 22. Splinting 23. Performance of ECGs 24. Interpretation of ECGs, chest radiographs, and extremity radiographs 25. POCUS 26. Mock Operating Room experience 27. Male and female urinary catheterization 28. Harvey the Cardiopulmonary Patient Simulator 29. SimMan 3G Plus High Fidelity Simulator 30. MamaAnne High Fidelity Simulator Applicants must submit the following materials electronically in PDF format: A curriculum vitae (CV) Academic transcripts Appropriate active certification and state license All application materials should be submitted through Interfolio , where applicants are also required to complete the Part-Time Mission Review as part of the process. For the 2026-2027 academic year, we will hire for immersion experiences taking place in September 2026, December 2026, April 2027, July 2027, and August 2027. For full consideration, you must apply by the following dates: September 2026 Immersion – apply by June 15, 2026 December 2026 Immersion – apply by August 24, 2026 April 2027 Immersion – apply by December 14, 2026 July 2027 Immersion – apply by March 15, 2027 August 2027 Immersion – apply by April 19, 2027 Hiring decisions will be made according to the following timeline: September 2026 Immersion – decisions made in June 2026 December 2026 Immersion – decisions made in September 2026 April 2027 Immersion – decisions made in January 2027 July 2027 Immersion – decisions made in April 2027 August 2027 Immersion – decisions made in May 2027 To explore current opportunities or access additional information, including answers to frequently asked questions, please visit the Adjunct Faculty FAQ on the Baylor PA Program website . For questions not addressed on the website please contact Adam Moore ( Adam_D_Moore@baylor.edu ), Academic Program Manager."
    }
  },
